// ⭐⭐ **THE COMPILER'S VIEW OF ONE OPEN BUFFER — the `Project` a document being edited makes, and the
// path that document is known by.**
//
// Two features ask for it and they must get the same one: `textDocument/completion` asks the corpus
// whether a member is VISIBLE from this file, and `textDocument/publishDiagnostics` asks the pipeline
// what is WRONG with it. Two constructions would be two projects free to disagree about which file the
// buffer even is, and a visibility answer is asked ON BEHALF OF a file.
//
// ⛔ **NOTHING IS READ FROM DISK, AND THAT IS FORCED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.** The document's text arrived
// over the wire, and in general the file does not exist on disk at all — an unsaved buffer, a uri a
// client invented. `loadProject` walks the filesystem for its sources, so it cannot serve here;
// `fileChanged` registers content directly with the query database, which is the same door
// `verify-recheck` edits a buffer through when it measures the editor scenario.
//
// ⚠ **ONE BUFFER, NOT A PROJECT ON DISK.** The server holds the open documents and nothing else, so the
// compile sees this file plus `stdlib/` — never the user's other sources. A diagnostic that depends on
// what a sibling file declares is therefore answered about a program the author did not write; the cure
// is project discovery, which this server does not have.

// `file:` — the one uri scheme a document this server checks can carry.
let LspFileUriScheme = "file://"

let LspUriRootSlash = "/"

let LspDriveLetterSeparator = ":"

// `/C` — the slash and the one letter in front of a `file:` uri's drive-letter colon.
let RootedDriveLetterLength = 2

// ⚠ **THE PATH IS AN IDENTITY, NOT A LOCATION — nothing ever opens it.** It answers `isVisibleFrom`'s
// question (is the file asking the same file that declared the member, and is it in the same module
// directory) and it is the compile ROOT, which is what a single-file `maxon build <file>` uses too;
// neither reads a byte from it. So the scheme is stripped and a Windows drive letter is un-rooted
// (`file:///C:/x` names `C:/x`, not `/C:/x`), and percent-escapes are left exactly as the client wrote
// them: two uris that differ by an escape name two different documents to this server either way.
module function lspDocumentPath(uri String) returns FilePath
	var text = uri

	if text.startsWith(LspFileUriScheme) 'fileScheme'
		text = text.replaceFirst(LspFileUriScheme, with: "")
	end 'fileScheme'

	if driveRooted(text) 'windowsDriveLetter'
		text = text.replaceFirst(LspUriRootSlash, with: "")
	end 'windowsDriveLetter'

	return try FilePath.from(text) otherwise panic("lspDocumentPath: '{uri}' does not spell a path, and every request that reaches here named a document this server has open")
end 'lspDocumentPath'

// `/C:/…` — a `file:` uri roots an absolute Windows path with a slash the path itself does not have.
function driveRooted(text String) returns bool
	let parts = text.split(LspDriveLetterSeparator)
	if parts.count() < 2 'noDriveLetter'
		return false
	end 'noDriveLetter'

	let head = try parts.get(0) otherwise panic("driveRooted: a split yielding two parts has a first one")
	return head.startsWith(LspUriRootSlash) and head.count() == RootedDriveLetterLength
end 'driveRooted'

// The project an open buffer makes, or nothing when this compiler cannot assemble one. A failure is a
// broken installation — no `stdlib/` beside the binary, or one that will not load — and each caller
// says what it does with the absence; there is no half-built project to hand back.
module union LspDocumentProject
	loaded(project Project)
	unavailable
end 'LspDocumentProject'

// A `Project` holding exactly this buffer plus `stdlib/`.
//
// ⚠ **THE ROOT IS THE DOCUMENT ITSELF, WHICH IS WHAT A SINGLE-FILE BUILD USES.**
// `Compiler.commonAncestorOf` hands one input path straight back, so `maxon build <file>` roots at the
// file — and every namespace this compile derives is measured from that root. A different root here
// would give the same buffer a different namespace under the editor than under a build.
module function lspDocumentProject(path FilePath, content String) returns LspDocumentProject
	let stdlibDir = try locateStdlibDir() otherwise return LspDocumentProject.unavailable
	var project = createProject(path, target: detectHostTarget(), stdlibDir: stdlibDir)

	fileChanged(project, path: path, content: content)

	try loadStdlibSources(project) otherwise return LspDocumentProject.unavailable
	return LspDocumentProject.loaded(project)
end 'lspDocumentProject'
